@@ -109,0 +110,27
+// Jep (co-op), coop-pt1108f (Jep's 11-09 playtest): retail's 72 blocks, then six only player 2's menu engine uses (74 to 79). Her menu sat in 44 to 49, and a town
+// script's _LOAD_TEXTURE family deletes and loads 45 to 49, so an event on her walk took her menu's pictures. 73 is never a block: the Z sheet's mark.
+// coop-eventslots (Jep's 11-11 item 1): 80 to 127, eight a player (six players), each player's own event's and fishing's blocks
+// (the fish, the fish caught, the bait, the event's 45 to 49), so two players' events and fishing never take each other's pictures
+// (port/src/coop/coop_eventslots.cpp CoopEventTexBlock).
+constexpr int kTextureBlockCount = 128;
+constexpr bool TextureBlockValid(int block) {
+    return block >= 0 && block < kTextureBlockCount && block != 73;
+}
+
+// Jep (co-op), coop-floorjoin2 (the host snapshot's "texture keys after an apply"): the
+// texture registry (render/texture_registry.hpp) re-keyed to the game's texture table after another copy's memory snapshot
+// was put back on this one (snapshot_state.cpp). The table (TexManager, in the state set) then names the host's TBP0 and
+// CBP keys, which the registry never handed out, so every draw missed. PortTextureRebuildBefore remembers this copy's own
+// entries by texture name; PortTextureRebuildAfterApply moves a fixed texture's entry (block -1: its pixels were never
+// staged) to the key the table now holds, drops the manager's other entries and makes each staged texture again at its keys
+// from its staged pixels (TextureData, in the state set). (Defined in game/texture.cpp.)
+struct PortTextureRebuildReport {
+    int images = 0;
+    int palettes = 0;
+    int targets = 0;
+    int moved = 0;
+    int skipped = 0;
+};
+void                     PortTextureRebuildBefore();
+PortTextureRebuildReport PortTextureRebuildAfterApply();
+
